---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/bp-sprite-animation-target.png";
import SheetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/bp-sprite-sheet.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Load = `sheet = love.graphics.newImage("sprite-sheet.png")
sheet:setFilter("nearest", "nearest")

for i = 1, 4 do
    frames[i] = love.graphics.newQuad(
        (i - 1) * 64, 0, 64, 64, sheet:getDimensions()
    )
end`;

const Advance = `timer = timer + dt

if timer > FRAME_TIME then
    timer = 0
    frame = frame + 1

    if frame > 4 then
        frame = 1
    end
end`;

const DrawIt = `love.graphics.draw(sheet, frames[frame], x, y, 0, 4 * facing, 4, 32, 32)`;

const Preview = `love.graphics.draw(sheet, 20, 20, 0, 2, 2)
love.graphics.setColor(1, 0.9, 0.2)
love.graphics.rectangle("line", 20 + (frame - 1) * 128, 20, 128, 128)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Bonus Project: Sprite Animation</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Everything you have drawn this quarter has
            been a rectangle or a circle.</b> This is where that stops. You load
            a picture, cut four frames out of it, and show them one after
            another fast enough that a character appears to walk.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>There is no game here.</b> There is a character, a floor, and two
            keys. What you get is the technique every 2D game in existence is
            built on, which is worth more than another dodging game.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is extra credit and it is optional.</b> Nothing later in the
            course depends on it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="before">
          <h2>Before You Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">You need everything through session 23.</b>{" "}
            Tables, loops, held-key input, and functions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Get the sprite sheet</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Right-click this image and save it</b> into your project folder
            as <CodeSnippet code="sprite-sheet.png" lang="txt" inline />. It is
            256 by 64 &mdash; four frames of 64 by 64, side by side, on a
            transparent background.
          </p>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image
              src={SheetImage}
              alt="A sprite sheet: four 64 by 64 frames of the same small character, arms and legs in four different walking positions"
              width={256}
              format="png"
              class="rounded bg-black p-2"
              style="image-rendering: pixelated; width: 512px; max-width: 100%;"
            />
          </div>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Draw your own instead if you would rather &mdash; anything four
            frames wide with square frames will work, and the code does not care
            what is in them. If you take one from the internet,{" "}
            <b>write down where it came from and whether you were allowed to use
            it.</b> That question does not go away when you leave this class.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Two new calls, and one new idea</h3>

          <div class="text-start">
            <dl>
              <dt><CodeSnippet code="love.graphics.newImage(path)" lang="lua" inline /></dt>
              <dd>Loads a picture from a file in your project folder. Do it <b>once</b>, in <CodeSnippet code="love.load" lang="lua" inline /> &mdash; never in <CodeSnippet code="love.draw" lang="lua" inline />.</dd>
              <dt><CodeSnippet code="love.graphics.newQuad(x, y, w, h, sw, sh)" lang="lua" inline /></dt>
              <dd>A <b>quad</b> is a rectangle cut out of an image. It is not a picture &mdash; it is instructions for which part of one to draw.</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The idea worth stopping on:</b> there is
            one image in memory, loaded once, and four little descriptions of
            where to look inside it. Animation is not four pictures. It is one
            picture and a number that changes.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> and
            rename it{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="bp-sprite-animation" lang="txt" inline />. The
            sprite sheet goes in that folder, next to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline />.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Build It</h2>

          <p>
            Five stages. <b class="text-sn-theme">Run it after every one.</b> A
            stage that works is worth more than three stages you cannot test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">1. Get one frame on screen</h3>

          <Code code={Load} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            Then draw quad number 1 somewhere and look at it.{" "}
            <b>If you see the whole sheet, you drew the image instead of the
            quad.</b> If you see nothing, the file is not in the folder or the
            name does not match &mdash; and LÖVE will have told you so on a blue
            screen.
          </p>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet code={`setFilter("nearest", "nearest")`} lang="lua" inline />{" "}
            is what keeps the pixels sharp when you scale it up. Leave it out
            once and see the difference; the blurry version is what every
            engine does by default and it is almost never what you want for
            pixel art.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">2. Cycle the frames</h3>

          <Code code={Advance} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This is a counter with a wrap</b>, which
            you have written before &mdash; it is the same shape as a loop
            index, except it lives across frames instead of inside one.{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="FRAME_TIME = 0.11" lang="lua" inline /> is about
            nine frames a second, which reads as a walk.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">3. Move, and face the right way</h3>

          <Code code={DrawIt} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            That call has a lot of arguments and every one earns its place:{" "}
            <b>the sheet, which quad, where, no rotation, the x scale, the y
            scale, and the point inside the frame that lands on x and y.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet code="facing" lang="lua" inline /> is 1 or &minus;1.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">A negative x scale flips the picture</b>,
            which is why you do not need a second set of frames for walking
            left &mdash; and why the last two arguments are 32, 32: it flips
            around its own middle rather than swinging off to one side.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">4. Stand still when you are standing still</h3>

          <p>
            Only advance the timer while a key is held. When nothing is pressed,
            snap back to frame 1.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Skip this and the character marches on the spot forever, which looks
            wrong in a way that is hard to name until you fix it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">5. Show your working</h3>

          <Code code={Preview} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            Draw the whole sheet in the corner at double size with a box round
            the frame currently in use.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This is a debugging tool that happens to
            look good</b>, and it makes the thing you cannot otherwise see
            &mdash; which frame is up &mdash; visible.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>What Success Looks Like</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image
              src={TargetImage}
              alt="A black 800 by 600 window with the four-frame sprite sheet across the top, a yellow box around the fourth frame, and the same character drawn large in the middle standing on a gray floor line"
              width={800}
              class="img-fluid rounded"
            />
          </div>

          <p>
            The four source frames across the top with the current one boxed,
            and the character drawn from that same frame at four times the size,
            mid-stride, walking right.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">One image file, one number, and the number
            is 4.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="extend">
          <h2>Then Make It Yours</h2>

          <ul>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Draw your own frames.</b> Any editor. Keep the frames square
              and the same size, and your code will not need a single change
              &mdash; which is the strongest possible argument for having
              written it with <CodeSnippet code="64" lang="lua" inline /> in one
              place rather than four.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Two animations.</b> A second row in the sheet for jumping or
              standing. The quad's y is what picks the row, and that is one more
              number.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Tie the speed to the movement.</b> Walk faster, cycle faster.
              One division. It is a tiny change and it looks enormously better.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Jump.</b> A vertical speed, gravity added every frame, and a
              floor to stop at. That is three lines and it is where a character
              turns into a platformer.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>A second character.</b> Same sheet, same quads, different
              position and frame number.{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Nothing is loaded twice</b>, and
              understanding why that is true is the whole point of quads.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Submit whatever you have.</b> A character that cycles four frames
            still earns credit, and so does a finished one with a bug you can
            describe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Project Complete
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
